All under the package `org.thingsboard.mqtt.broker`. The cache infrastructure lives in the `common/cache` module; the three service classes live in the `application` module.

## How caching works in this codebase

This project does **not** use Spring's declarative annotations (`@Cacheable`/`@CacheEvict`/`@CachePut`) anywhere in main code. Instead it uses **programmatic cache access** via a `CacheManager` wrapper.

**Configuration** (`TbCaffeineCacheConfiguration.java`): Defines a `CacheManager` bean (Caffeine-backed, or Redis via `TBRedisCacheConfiguration`). It's `@ConditionalOnProperty(prefix = "cache", value = "type", havingValue = "caffeine", matchIfMissing = true)` and annotated `@EnableCaching`. Caches are built from `CacheSpecsMap` (bound to the `cache.specs.*` YAML config) — each spec has `timeToLiveInMinutes` and `maxSize`. The manager is wrapped in a `TransactionAwareCacheManagerProxy`.

**Access** (`CacheNameResolver.java`): This is the single access point. It wraps `CacheManager` and prepends a configurable prefix:
```java
@Component @Data
public class CacheNameResolver {
    private final CacheManager cacheManager;
    @Value("${cache.cache-prefix:}")
    private String cachePrefix;

    public Cache getCache(String cacheName) {
        return cacheManager.getCache(cachePrefix + cacheName);
    }
}
```

**Usage pattern**: Services inject `CacheNameResolver` and fetch a `org.springframework.cache.Cache` by name, then call `.get()`/`.put()` directly. Example from `BasicMqttClientAuthProvider.java`:
```java
private Cache getBasicCredsPwCache() {
    return cacheNameResolver.getCache(CacheConstants.BASIC_CREDENTIALS_PASSWORD_CACHE);
}
// ...
getBasicCredsPwCache().put(toHashString(password), credentials);
```
Other examples of the same pattern: `MqttClientCredentialsServiceImpl` (dao), `DevicePacketIdAndSerialNumberServiceImpl` (dao), `SslMqttClientAuthProvider`, `DevicePersistenceProcessorImpl`.

## How cache constants are defined (`CacheConstants.java`)

Constants are plain `public static final String` cache-name keys, some composed with suffixes:
```java
public static final String PACKET_ID_AND_SERIAL_NUMBER_CACHE = "packetIdAndSerialNumber";
public static final String MQTT_CLIENT_CREDENTIALS_CACHE = "mqttClientCredentials";
public static final String BASIC_CREDENTIALS_PASSWORD_CACHE = "basicCredentialsPassword";
public static final String SSL_REGEX_BASED_CREDENTIALS_CACHE = "sslRegexBasedCredentials";
public static final String CLIENT_SESSIONS_LIMIT_CACHE = "clientSessionsLimit";
public static final String APP_CLIENTS_LIMIT_CACHE = "appClientsLimit";

public static final String BUCKET_SUFFIX = "::bucket";
public static final String DEVICE_PERSISTED_MSGS_LIMIT_CACHE = "devicePersistedMsgsLimit" + BUCKET_SUFFIX;
public static final String TOTAL_MSGS_LIMIT_CACHE = "totalMsgsLimit" + BUCKET_SUFFIX;

public static final String COUNT_SUFFIX = "::count";
public static final String CLIENT_SESSIONS_LIMIT_CACHE_KEY = CacheConstants.CLIENT_SESSIONS_LIMIT_CACHE + COUNT_SUFFIX;
public static final String APP_CLIENTS_LIMIT_CACHE_KEY = CacheConstants.APP_CLIENTS_LIMIT_CACHE + COUNT_SUFFIX;
```
Each string cache name (e.g. `"packetIdAndSerialNumber"`) has a matching entry under `cache.specs.*` in `/workspace/repo/application/src/main/resources/thingsboard-mqtt-broker.yml` (around line 813) defining its TTL and max size.

Related supporting files if useful: `CacheSpecsMap.java` and `CacheSpecs.java` (same `common/cache` package), and `TBRedisCacheConfiguration.java` for the Redis alternative. Nothing was modified."}

